  <h2>Sports Bra Design Starts with Support Level</h2>
  <p>A sports bra is not only a style item. It is a support product that must control movement, protect comfort and keep its shape after repeated wear. Before sampling, brands should define whether the product is low-impact, medium-impact or high-impact.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Support Level</th>
        <th>Typical Use</th>
        <th>Manufacturing Focus</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Low impact</td>
        <td>Yoga, pilates, studio wear</td>
        <td>Soft hand feel, comfortable straps, clean neckline</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Medium impact</td>
        <td>Training, gym sessions, daily activewear</td>
        <td>Balanced compression, stable underband, breathable panels</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>High impact</td>
        <td>Running, HIIT, intense workouts</td>
        <td>Stronger recovery, wider straps, secure cup and underband structure</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Key Construction Decisions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Compression vs encapsulation:</strong> compression works well for simpler studio bras, while molded or separated cup structures improve support for larger size ranges.</li>
    <li><strong>Underband stability:</strong> the underband should hold without rolling, digging or losing recovery after washing.</li>
    <li><strong>Strap design:</strong> wider or adjustable straps improve support and reduce shoulder pressure.</li>
    <li><strong>Back structure:</strong> racerback, cross-back and hook-and-eye options change support, comfort and styling.</li>
    <li><strong>Lining and pads:</strong> removable pads, fixed pads or molded cups should match the brand's target use case.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fabric and Elastic Requirements</h2>
  <p>For high-support bras, Fitclo usually recommends dense nylon/spandex or polyester/spandex fabrics with strong recovery. Power mesh can be added in wings or back panels for extra hold and breathability. Elastic quality is also critical because weak elastic causes underband rolling and fit complaints.</p>

  <h2>Sample Approval Checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Check bounce control during movement, not only on a mannequin.</li>
    <li>Stretch straps and underband repeatedly to test recovery.</li>
    <li>Wash the sample and remeasure key points.</li>
    <li>Confirm pad shape, lining comfort and seam position.</li>
    <li>Review logo placement so branding does not crack, peel or irritate skin.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fitclo Recommendation</h2>
  <p>If you are launching your first sports bra collection, start with one low-to-medium impact style and one medium-to-high support style. This gives your brand two clear customer use cases while keeping sample development focused.</p>
